From 6d298fbd7ffb8b092cd99d7f0e9447dcd8954b18 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 18:14:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] dvbridge: keep the EGL images of the enhancement-layer
 surfaces

Every frame with an enhancement layer mapped its VAAPI surface to DRM
PRIME, made two EGL images and two textures from it, looked up three EGL
entry points and the EGL extension string, and destroyed all of it after
the render. The decoder has a fixed pool of surfaces, so the same images
can serve again, as Kodi's own VAAPI texture pool does for the base layer
(CVaapiTexturePool).

dvbridge_dmabuf_import_cached() still maps the frame every time (FFmpeg
waits for the decoder there and exports the surface), and identifies the
memory by the device and inode of each dma-buf and by the whole layout
given to EGL; a surface seen before gets its images and textures back.
Frames of another pool of surfaces, or another EGL display, empty the
cache; when it is full (32 surfaces) the least recently used one makes
room. The cache of a bridge is emptied at the end of native output and
destroyed with the bridge; how many imports found their images and how
many made them is logged then.

Tested on the host with EGL, OpenGL ES and av_hwframe_map() replaced by
counting fakes over memfds (a new descriptor of the same file for every
mapping, as vaExportSurfaceHandle gives): the same surface gives the same
textures without new images, another dma-buf behind the same surface id,
another pool or another display do not, the least recently used surface
makes room, a failed import leaves nothing, every image and texture is
deleted exactly once, every mapping is released, and without a cache the
import behaves as before. Not run on a GPU: whether a driver gives the
same dma-buf for a surface every time (which Kodi's base-layer pool relies
on as well) is seen in the log line of the cache.
